 the current microbiology lab  manager at houston methodist  dr long and ms turnock you may now begin  your presentation  all right thank you very much and thanks  everybody for joining us today um  look forward to talking to you about our  experience with the kystra and  what some of the benefits have been  for us here in the laboratory in houston  methodist  so just first in terms of disclosures i  don't have any relevant conflicts of  interest to disclose  at this point  um  and i think for most people who are  interested in you know lab automation  systems like the bd keystroke one of the  first  questions you are confronted with is  making a business case for each  administration  because uh  these systems are not obviously uh  inexpensive they are large capital  acquisitions  and  uh  you know as such require leadership  support  and buy-in and significant capital  resources to acquire  uh and hospitals have limited resources  i think we all can appreciate that  um in the modern age and that you know  to make the business case you're gonna  have to  uh generally generate some sort of  justification or roi  for your leadership to  justify the allocation of the capital  resources you need to acquire an  automation system  and so i think the question that  confronts  any of us certainly confronted us here  he's the methodist  back  years ago and we've started this process  and will confront anyone who goes to  acquire when these systems is how can  you justify  the acquisition of a large automation  line  or upgrading an automation line if you  have one already that you've outgrown  and so  uh one of the common ways that people  will  uh suggest that you try to  justify acquisitions like an automation  lag will be uh in terms of ftes and the  theoretical reduction in ftes  uh and that's usually what maybe your  bench technologists are worried about is  are people gonna get laid off  um  that sort of question and so we sort of  confronted that head on uh in the ve