 six months review it  and  then  review those new duties with all of the  employees  everybody is held accountable for their  responsibilities  one of the things you may want to  re-examine is spot testing do you uh if  you are going to do spot testing is the  reader going to do it or is the task  analyst going to do it  we tried uh eliminating the spot testing  like indoles for  e coli and that but it kind of  overwhelmed the person with the maldives  and actually we found that it was  delaying  identifications so we re-implemented  the spot testing and we saw a decrease  in the turnaround time on the  identifications  sometimes it's trial and error  remember that every time you pull the  plates out of the incubator you are  cutting into the incubation time so you  really have to work with the staff on  practicing with the images put any kind  of organism you can think of on plates  put them in the incubator and let them  view what the colonies look like  and you do need to remember to let the  employees have a voice in defining the  workflow  they know it better than you do as a  manager or as a pathologist  and they will accept the changes much  better if you actually let them  participate in it  when you are getting ready to go live we  have to make sure that everyone gets a  chance to be the reader or the task  analyst or the inocula before the system  goes live with that type of culture we  made sure everybody could do the urines  on all areas  got that down well we probably  had urines going for about three to four  months before we started on the second  type of cultures which were sterile body  fluids  um  and that worked to our advantage because  everybody felt pretty comfortable with  it  because we made sure that everybody got  to be a reader and a task analyst  the employees  made  suggestions on workflow changes  constantly we had to kind of say we've  got to have a way to for everybody to  turn in suggestions for the workflow  changes for us to evaluate them and then  get back to the employees as to why it  worked or why it didn't work  again you have to work with your nursing  education about the collection  containers for each set of cultures that  you are ready to to bring on  it when you're going to bring the next  set of cultures on  is the employees that are are the  employees that are going to be doing it  are they learning a new skill  all of our employees um knew how all of  our  mlts and um mls's knew how to read urine  cultures  but the m uh the evening and night shift  did not know how to read sterile body  fluids or respiratories or anything else  so when we started on the next set of  cultures i.e sterile body fluids  we uh made sure that we started with  these sterile body fluids because they  would have some positive but not a  tremendous number of positives and it  would ease them in to be able to looking  at cultures with um  broths and examining the cultures on  multiple days  uh the positive blood cultures were  included in this and uh i was really  pleased with how this  went with the employees that had no  did not have skills in reading the other  cultures uh it was more of a gradual  approach to it and they did quite well  it took us about three to four months  again to get  all of them trained and uh on reading  the cultures and then  implementing the sterile body fluids  validate your cultures again with  specific reading times  um train the employees and let them  practice practice and practice i can't  emphasize enough how important letting  the employees have time on the  instrument um and  to so that they can train their eyes  make sure everyone gets uh practice on  again the reading the and the uh ta part  and again they we redefine the duties of  the tas every time we put on a new  culture type  those duties and that sometimes changed  oh once it's live again make sure that  uh before you're thinking about your  third set as we did  you could do  two uh go lives or four go live  but make sure everybody gets a chance to  be the reader and the ta and the inocula  tech before you go on to your next one  you think you thought of everything you  just knew it but then reality heads  and  usually you've thought of all the major  issues but it's the little things that  um you don't think about and those are  the ones that the employees will come up  with well  we didn't decide where this uh racks  were going to go in the incubator it's  confusing which shift is doing them so  it's those types of thing that we had to  address later  in the last go live we just repeated the  steps from previous ones we started off  with the respiratory cultures having  images taken at a longer time  essentially 24 hours and we have not  changed that that seems to work really  well for us on those respiratory  cultures we don't have to send out a a  report that says it's too young we're  going to you know read it later  we usually have something very visible  to grow with 24 hours  and you also have to consider those  cultures that are not going to go on the  keystroke are you going to inoculate  them on the keister in the semi  automated mode or are you going to  inoculate them manually for instance  like your anaerobic cultures if you have  any other plated cultures  iv catheter tips or something like that  some of the lessons that we learned were  essentially one of the most challenging  areas was the inocula specimens that had  afb and fungus cultures and aerobic and  anaerobic cultures we found we couldn't  split those specimens you actually had  to take the specimen like bronchial  washings and do everything in a manual  mode it got too confusing for the  employees  we did find that inoculation of cultures  that don't incubate on the keystroke  works extremely well they just go to a  bin we pick them up put them in the  anaerobe bins  great utilize it as much as you can  there are problems in keeping the work  on the lis the recording results on the  lis and the marking on the kystra make  sure that you outline what goes on each  the workflow documentation  you just have to keep on people  planning ahead is absolutely essential  and anything that you can do beforehand  making changes rotational reading  cultures  containers that all will help  one of the things that we did not  anticipate that the employees told us  about was that  it  the employees felt differently about  reading the cultures they felt that in  the old way when you were handling the  cultures themselves that they had a  feeling of accomplishment when they went  home they did a day's work they left  feeling good i got it all done  when they leave now  they don't have quite that same feeling  because they may have just read the  cultures and there's still some that are  have to be read because it's so  continuous and it's um a little bit  different in a mentality so that did  affect the employees in that way  making sure everybody knows how to  troubleshoot it that is a requirement  here you have to troubleshoot it  yourself  be sure and listen to the employees  they've come up with absolutely  fantastic ideas  and it is stressful so uh  we celebrated a lot when we had you know  when the urines went through and we were  uh at the end of that right before we  went to the  next culture type we did celebrate you  have to make sure that you do  and yes we would do it again  so that's my presentation thank you very  much for your attendance and um  i think that now that  we'll be entertaining questions  thank you dr long and ms chernock for  your informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now just  click on the ask a question box located  on the far left of your screen we'll  answer as many of your questions as we  have time for let's get started  our first question is  how has the kiastra system helped your  lab during covid  i wanted to i was just i saw this  question at first and i thought well i  mean really it didn't run covet tests so  it wasn't that helpful but actually one  of the things that i would say was  helpful was  uh it since it changed our workflow and  we went to a situation you know before  we had the kystra  uh there were certain shifts you know in  the middle of the night where plates  maybe weren't being read  uh and not all technologists uh  were reading plates there there was  cross training but there wasn't  the same sort of cross training is once  we move to the keystroke we move to  you know the automation system and  plates were being you know coming ready  to be read 24 7 we had to train more of  our text in reading plates and do other  things and so i think because of that at  least in my perception  during covid when we went through peaks  where we had multiple technologists who  were out unfortunately either with covet  or with family members code et cetera  um i think it made us a little bit more  flexible in terms of our labor and how  we could have other techs pick up the  slack  to do plate reading other things i think  i think in that case the sort of the way  it changed our workflow and our  crosstraining helped  right thank you  our next question is it possible to have  incubators at different atmospheres eg  ambient versus five percent co2  yes we do have uh two co2 incubators and  three ambient air  incubators on the  keestra  all right  next question  how did the incubation decrease impact  your staffing models assuming maldi tof  changed it in the first place and this  would have changed it again  we actually didn't change our  staffing models for the culture reading  we  we did try some employees coming in  earlier um for a while and some staying  later  but we found that um  it didn't help  it really actually hurt  more so than it helped we kept our  regular three shifts  um when we actually  were able to get some new positions  because of increased workload  we moved them to the shifts that had the  heaviest reader time  and  that helped us even it out a little  all right  next question  how do you deal with clsi's organism age  requirements for susceptibility testing  we did a validation on identification on  how old the colonies were if they were  um  a lot of times your colonies are about  12 hours old when you  pick them  and we did susceptibility testing at  that time that we picked them  which is usually even though it's 12  hours when you read them by the time you  pick them it may be  12 hours  and then at the  routine time of about 24 hours and we  did not see  any significant difference  for instance there was no  interpretation difference in the  susceptibilities  thank you  next question  have you considered having text work  from home when reading plates from the  computer  i marked this one i i wanted to say uh  it's certainly something i have i have  thought about  um  you know there's certain uh  cap requirements and and regulations uh  related to digital pathology and there's  been certain relaxations  um  you know i think to do it properly you  would probably have to issue  technologists a standard uh you know the  monitor on the keystroke is a fairly  high definition monitor i think you  would have to replicate that  at home uh to have some consistency but  it is  we live in houston we have you know  hurricanes and freezes and other things  where it makes it difficult for people  to come into work um and it you know it  is  it is in some ways  attractive to me uh as a medical  director to think about possibilities of  you know is there a way we could have  people  uh read place from home and i think i  think that future may be coming  but i do think there's just some  logistics to be worked out in terms of  you know standardization monitors and um  and how to operationalize it um  in terms of you know uh  paying for the employees and and how  that you know would they how would you  pay the employees that are off-site and  how would you you know track the work  but i do think it is i do think it's a  tantalizing possibility with the system  that may be on the horizon i don't know  how far it is out there  i did can tell you that um i have the  system on my  um  computer in the office and they'll mark  it uh for a manager review and it's so  nice to be able to just to view it here  and not have to go into the laboratory  and i can make comments on it and it  works out great  in that respect  okay great thank you  next question  did you look at the copan wasp lab2 what  were the reasons benefits that led you  to go with the bd kystra  uh i would say just off the top my  recollection is when we looked at it in  our initial uh  for our volumes and our throughput um  once they did the final we we did  discuss with both vendors and that the  copan the wasp system came back and we  were going to need two separate wasp  lines  and by the time  we were going to have enough i guess the  equivalent wasp system was really going  to be not  that much uh less expensive than the  keystra and  for that reason  um that was one of the major drivers for  me and then also we're just we're also  you know full disclosure a fairly beady  heavy operation with  instrumentation and so um we had that  pre-existing vendor relationship  uh and so that made the decision a  little easier too but um  you know i think that for me and i was  associate director at the time but that  was one of the major  things was once we finally did the final  calculations it wasn't actually all that  much before it wasn't all that much more  cheaper than the keister system that met  our needs  right thank you  next question  can you share a little information about  the demographics in your lab employees  um yes um our employees uh here in the  laboratory  it's probably  um  less than 60 we probably have about 80  percent of our employees that are less  than 60  uh and the remaining um 20  or over 60.  the majority of the older employees  