r any specific  notifications about our keester go lives  because it was uh  for  the most part it was fairly seamless for  our clinicians  all right  next question have you been able to  reduce the workload for employees even  without looking to reduce total fte  counts  i don't think our employee workload has  gone down because our overall workload  is pretty much increased  um  continue how would you think  our workload in has increased  so dramatically  in 21  that i have gotten six new positions  so i i don't think that that question is  relevant for us  all right we just have too much organic  growth in the system uh to really gauge  that  okay well it looks like we have time for  one more question  how many times did you present your  justification to leadership to attain  it um i would say to me it felt like  several times we kind of we started  initial discussions  uh  there was money allocated for sort of us  some architectural studies and analysis  and and  feasibility studies and and looking at  you know as part of a larger move our  lab from one building to another  uh with automation in mind and then  there was  um we sort of went forward at one point  and then stopped for some more financial  reviews coming out of leadership and  provided more justification and then  after that period  uh it finally got approved and  after code everything pre-code was a  little fuzzy but there was sort of a  couple of years of starts and stops and  multiple justifications we went through  before we finally got the  full ticket price approved  it was on the third year like dr long  says and for such a huge expenditure for  us  that's about how long it usually takes  us  well thank you again dr long and ms  churnock for your time today and your  important research we would also like to  thank lab roots and our sponsor bd for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  